
 distributed.net client for Tenstorrent Blackhole devices
 document revision $Id: readme.tenstorrent $

 Welcome to the distributed.net client.

 This document covers information specific to the client for Tenstorrent
 Blackhole accelerators, and for running it on the ttsim simulator when
 no such device is available. Refer to other enclosed documentation or
 browse the online FAQ at <http://faq.distributed.net/> for
 non-platform-specific documentation.

    1.0  Getting started
    2.0  Tenstorrent specific notes
         2.1 Cores
         2.2 Running on the ttsim simulator
         2.3 Self-checks
         2.4 Environment variables
    3.0  Building
         3.1 Device firmware
    4.0  Troubleshooting and known issues

 1.0  Getting started ------------------------------------------------

    Just unpack/unzip/untar the client in a directory of your choice and
    fire it up.

    If you have never run the client before, it will initiate the
    menu-driven configuration. Save and quit when done.

    Then, simply restart the client. From that point on it will use the
    saved configuration.

    The configuration options are fairly self-explanatory and can be run
    at any time by starting the client with the '-config' option.
    A list of command line options is available by starting the client
    with '-help' or '--help'.

    A complete step-by-step guide to running your first client is
    available at <https://www.distributed.net/Docs_tutor_client>


 2.0  Tenstorrent specific notes -------------------------------------

    The client supports RC5-72 on Tenstorrent Blackhole devices (p100,
    p150 and similar cards) on Linux. It needs the Tenstorrent kernel
    driver (tt-kmd) with exclusive device ownership support, and read/write
    access to the device nodes in /dev/tenstorrent.

    The client takes exclusive ownership of each device it uses. Do not
    run it at the same time as other programs using the device (such as
    TT-Metalium); the client reports devices in use and skips them. When it
    exits, the client stops its firmware on the device.

    To list the devices detected by the client, run "dnetc -gpuinfo".
    Every Blackhole device found is used, one cruncher per device; see
    "Multiple GPUs" in readme.opencl about the "-devicenum" option, which
    works the same way here.

  2.1   Cores ------------------------------------------------------

    Every Tensix tile of the device has five small RISC-V cores and a
    32-lane vector unit (SFPU). The client loads its own firmware onto all
    of the tiles. There are two RC5-72 cores:

    #0 "RISC-V x5": all five RISC-V cores of every tile check keys.
    #1 "SFPU + RISC-V x4": one RISC-V core of every tile drives its vector
       unit, checking 32 keys at a time; the other four check keys
       themselves. This is the default and is much faster.

  2.2   Running on the ttsim simulator ----------------------------------

    ttsim is Tenstorrent's simulator, which presents a virtual Blackhole
    device. It is much slower than a real device, but bit-exact, so the
    client's tests can be run without Tenstorrent hardware. Download the
    Blackhole simulator library, libttsim_bh.so, from
    <https://github.com/tenstorrent/ttsim/releases> and point the client
    at it:

        DNETC_TTSIM=/path/to/libttsim_bh.so ./dnetc -test rc5-72

    With DNETC_TTSIM set, the client uses the simulator instead of any
    devices in /dev/tenstorrent. "-test" takes about 30 seconds per core
    on the simulator; "-stress" takes hours.

  2.3   Self-checks --------------------------------------------------

    Before a core is first used, the client checks it on every tile of the
    device: each tile must find a key planted in a range of keys that
    crosses a carry in the key, and the vector unit's result for every key
    is compared with the RISC-V cores' result. A failure is reported and
    stops the client. Any key found by the device is also re-checked on the
    host before it is reported.

  2.4   Environment variables --------------------------------------------

    DNETC_TTSIM=path      use the ttsim simulator library at 'path' instead
                          of the devices in /dev/tenstorrent
    DNETC_TT_MAX_TILES=n  use at most n Tensix tiles per device, for
                          bring-up and debugging
    DNETC_TT_STATS=1      log device clock statistics for every core call


 3.0  Building -------------------------------------------------------

    On Linux x86_64:

        ./configure linux-amd64-tenstorrent
        make

    No Tenstorrent software is needed to build the client.

  3.1   Device firmware ----------------------------------------------

    The firmware the client loads onto the device is built from
    rc5-72/tenstorrent/r72tt_tile.c. Its image is included in the source as
    rc5-72/tenstorrent/r72tt_tile_fw.cpp, so that building the client
    does not need a RISC-V compiler. After changing the firmware sources,
    regenerate the image with the SFPI toolchain
    <https://github.com/tenstorrent/sfpi/releases> (version 7.90.0 was used
    for the included image):

        SFPI_PATH=/path/to/sfpi ./configure linux-amd64-tenstorrent
        make tt-firmware

    Rebuilding the image from unchanged sources with the same SFPI version
    reproduces r72tt_tile_fw.cpp exactly.


 4.0  Troubleshooting and known issues ------------------------------

    "is in use by another program": another program has the device open.
    Stop it and retry.

    "does not support exclusive device ownership": the kernel driver is too
    old; update tt-kmd.

    If a device stops responding, it can be reset with "tt-smi -r"
    <https://github.com/tenstorrent/tt-smi>.
